    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d602">

                <pb facs="0896" n="790" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg790"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.458/14: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-11-04T15:00:00-05:00">November 4, 1939—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>228. No message of felicitation will be sent direct by the President on November
                    7, the Soviet national holiday.<anchor n="95" xml:id="d602-f1"/> There is no
                    reason, however, why you should not convey customary felicitations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d602-f1" n="95" xml:id="d602-f1-content">
                <p>In a memorandum of October 31, 1939, by George T. Summerlin, Chief of the
                    Division of Protocol, he wrote that, in view of present conditions, “regardless
                    of the manner in which the telegram is drafted there may be more adverse comment
                    than usual.” The Chief of the Division of European Affairs, Pierrepont Moffat,
                    noted upon this: “Given the recent inexplicable attitude toward us of the Soviet
                    authorities, I am inclined this year to omit a congratulatory telegram &amp; to
                    confine ourselves to having cards left at the Embassy here.” (861.458/14)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
